AquaStream360 Software Overview

AquaStream360 is a comprehensive software platform designed to support the modeling, analysis, and evaluation of water distribution networks. The application enables users to perform complex analyses and network modifications in a manner that significantly enhances decision-making quality, analytical accuracy, operational efficiency, and contextual relevance across the entire workflow.
The software is largely automated by design. Most features do not require manual installation, configuration, or complex setup procedures. Instead, users can typically interact with the system through direct actions such as clicking, viewing, analyzing, modifying, or exporting results. This automation allows users to focus on interpretation and decision-making rather than tool configuration.
________________________________________
I.	Self-Guided and Interactive Design
AquaStream360 is built around a self-guided interaction model. The application communicates with users primarily through visual cues and, optionally, audio feedback. Color-based indicators are used extensively throughout the interface to highlight required actions, incomplete steps, and areas of potential relevance based on the user’s current objective.
All features within the application are enabled by default. When a user interacts with any control, the system responds in one of two ways:
•	The intended action is executed immediately, or
•	The system communicates which prerequisite steps must be completed before the action can proceed, typically by highlighting the relevant interface elements in red.
When the speech option is enabled (accessible from the top-right area of the Modeling section controls), the application can also provide audio feedback to further guide the user through required steps or important actions.
________________________________________
II.	Network-Driven Workflow
All functionality within AquaStream360 is based on the presence of a network model. If a network has not yet been generated or imported, any attempt to use analysis or visualization features prompts the application to inform the user that a network is required. The system then automatically guides the user to the appropriate network generation or import tools.
Once a network has been created, simulation becomes the next critical stage in the workflow. If simulation has not been executed, the application periodically highlights the simulation control (approximately every 15 seconds) to prompt the user to complete this step. Running a simulation unlocks a wide range of analytical, visualization, and diagnostic features throughout the software.
________________________________________
III.	Georeferencing and Spatial Enablement
Georeferencing is a fundamental requirement for many advanced capabilities within AquaStream360. When a network is not georeferenced, certain spatial and GIS-based features may be unavailable. In such cases, the software automatically highlights the georeferencing option and guides the user through the required process.
AquaStream360 significantly simplifies georeferencing. Users are not required to possess GIS expertise, engineering survey knowledge, or external software tools, nor is extensive manual effort necessary. The application provides automated georeferencing functionality that allows users to geo-reference a network in seconds. This can be achieved by supplying the coordinates of any two known reference points or by interactively selecting corresponding locations on the map. The system then automatically recalculates spatial coordinates for all remaining network elements.
________________________________________
IV.	Built-In Guidance and Modular Design
Guidance for many core features is embedded directly within the software. AquaStream360 actively communicates with users through interface feedback, ensuring that sufficient instruction is provided to achieve desired outcomes without constant reliance on external documentation.
The platform includes advanced visual analysis capabilities that enable users to examine network performance through:
•	Graph-based analytical plots
•	Flow animations
•	Animated color-coded performance maps
•	GIS-based animated visualizations
•	Three-dimensional (3D) network representations
Many of these capabilities are implemented as standalone modules. When activated, these modules open as independent environments and incorporate their own internal guidance mechanisms, allowing users to interact with them efficiently and intuitively.
The interface employs clear labeling and logical grouping of controls, ensuring that navigation remains accessible even within a large and feature-rich environment.
________________________________________
V.	Integrated GIS Capabilities
AquaStream360 incorporates advanced GIS functionality that is deeply integrated into the platform. Some GIS-related features are activated contextually and appear only when relevant to the current workflow. In addition, the Analysis section includes a dedicated GIS-based environment that enables users to construct water distribution networks from scratch, define zones, and perform spatial operations using the same core analytical algorithms that underpin the rest of the application.
________________________________________
Typical Usage Workflow
A typical workflow within AquaStream360 follows these general steps:
1.	Generate a network or load an example network
2.	Simulate the network
3.	Analyze, modify, or test the network using automated tools designed to support efficient, effective, and meaningful decision-making












Main Application Structure
The software is divided into three main subsections: Modeling, Analysis, and Monitoring. Each subsection provides a distinct set of tools while remaining tightly integrated with the rest of the application.
a.	Modeling Section
Despite being referred to as the Modeling section, this part of the software offers a comprehensive set of features that also support analysis and network evaluation. It serves as the central hub of the application and provides shortcuts to most modules available across the entire software.
The Modeling section includes modules and tools dedicated to the following tasks:
•	Network modification and editing
•	Manual network drawing
•	File import and data ingestion
•	Controls configuration
•	Network and results analysis
•	Mapping and geospatial visualization
•	Data filtering
•	Tabulation of simulation results
•	Generation of network models from diverse data sources
•	Advanced visualizations
•	Network zoning
•	Water quality configuration and options
•	Network animations (2d and 3d animations)
•	Exporting results or network models to different files formats



b.	Analysis Module
The Analysis module focuses on advanced network analysis and bulk editing of network elements. It enables users to examine network attributes in detail and then apply targeted modifications based on defined conditions.
The Analysis module includes several sections and sub-sections, including:
•	Modify Elements: Allows users to define conditions and apply bulk modifications to network elements.
•	Performance Investigation: Enables users to test multiple scenarios and assess how different maintenance strategies or operational scenarios impact the network. Simulations can be executed without permanently modifying the parent network, allowing safe experimentation unless changes are explicitly applied.
•	Resilience Analysis: Evaluates the network’s ability to withstand and recover from failures or adverse conditions.
•	Change Analysis: Identifies and analyzes changes that occur during network modification processes.
•	Criticality Analysis: Determines the importance and impact of individual network elements on overall system performance.
•	Geospatial Analysis: Provides spatial-based analysis tools to evaluate network behavior and attributes geographically.
•	Zoning Analysis: Supports the creation and assessment of network zones for operational and analytical purposes.
c.	Monitoring Module
The Monitoring module is designed for both real-time and historical analysis of network performance. It provides advanced monitoring capabilities that allow users to track system behavior over time.
Although this module is still under active development, it already includes features for real-time performance monitoring and tracking, and it is expected to evolve further with additional analytical and visualization capabilities. This module will not be explained in this document, as its features are still changing.













A.	MODELLING SECTION IN DETAIL 

AquaStream360 Modeling Module: Top Frame (Left-Side Controls) Documentation
The top frame of the Modeling module is a multi-row command ribbon containing the primary tools for network construction, modification, and preparation for analysis. The controls on the left side are logically grouped to guide the user from fundamental network creation to advanced operational and resilience assessments. Below is a detailed breakdown of each functional group and its features.
________________________________________
1. Network Construction and Data Import
These foundational tools allow users to create a network from scratch or import it from multiple data sources.
•	Network Builder: Primary entry point for model creation. Opens an intelligent dialogue that can automatically generate a calibrated hydraulic network model from diverse file formats, including Excel, CSV, GIS shapefiles, CAD drawings, or database connections. The system interprets data structure, identifies element types and attributes, and establishes connectivity with minimal user input, guided by on-screen prompts.
•	Import Elements: Adds new physical components (pipes, nodes, valves, pumps, tanks) to an existing network model. Imports data files and auto-adds elements along with their attributes, integrating them into the current network topology.
•	Import Attributes: Updates the properties of existing network elements without altering the physical structure. Imports data files containing new attribute values (e.g., diameter, roughness, status) and automatically updates only those elements whose names are matched, leaving others unchanged.
________________________________________
2. Material and Attribute Management
Tools for defining, modifying, and managing properties and characteristics of network components.
•	Material Properties: Define a standardized library of pipe material properties. Set default values for Hazen-Williams C-factor, roughness, cost, and diameter ranges. Defaults are applied automatically during network creation, simulation or element addition if attributes are not specified.
•	Attr Modifier: Bulk editing tool enabling modifications of attributes for multiple elements simultaneously, based on conditions, a selected list, or graphical map selection.
•	Custom Attr: Extend the standard data schema by defining custom attributes, critical for specialized analyses like pipe integrity management (working stress, corrosion rate, installation year, failure probability).
•	Group Editing: Spatial selection and batch modification interface. Users can draw a region on the map to select all elements and uniformly modify attributes or apply changes based on individual properties.
________________________________________
3. Pattern Definition and Editing
Manage temporal variations in network demands, operations, and other time-based parameters.
•	Add Patterns: Create and visualize time-based patterns (e.g., demand, energy cost) before adding to the network. Supports manual input, file import, or built-in templates, with graphical previews of the pattern curve.
•	Edit Patterns: Modify and manually optimize existing patterns, adjusting multipliers, extending periods, or normalizing patterns through an intuitive interface.
•	Analyze Patterns: Graphical environment for analyzing, comparing, and automatically optimizing existing patterns. Uses advanced smoothing techniques and statistical analysis for accurate simulation.
________________________________________
4. Controls, Curves, and Thresholds
Define operational logic, system characteristics, and performance boundaries.
•	Add Controls: Define operational rules and logic for components like pumps and valves. Conditional statements based on time, tank level, or pressure govern element operation during simulation.
•	Add Curves: Define and manage characteristic curves (pump curves, efficiency vs. flow, etc.). Input data points, import from files, or select from a library; system generates continuous curves for simulation.
•	Set Thresholds: Establish minimum, maximum, and target performance thresholds for parameters like pressure, flow velocity, or water quality. Thresholds are used to color-code results, trigger warnings, and generate reports.
________________________________________
5. Calibration, Failure, and Resilience Tools
Advanced modules for model refinement and robustness assessment.
•	Net Calibrator: This opens an Automated calibration suite adjusting model parameters (pipe roughness, demand) to match field observations (pressure, flow) using optimization algorithms.
•	Failure Analysis: Analyze the vulnerability and integrity risks of pipes based on simulated operating condition.
•	Pipe Failure Test: This opens a popup window for  assessment of pipe integrity through hoop stress calculations, safety factor evaluation, 3D visualization, and comparative analysis across multiple pipes. Users can simulate “what-if” scenarios in a sandbox environment to evaluate potential risks and failure probabilities without altering the original network.
•	Resilience: This opens the resilience section allowing the user to quantify the network's ability to withstand and recover from disruptions. Calculates resilience indices and tests improvement strategies’ impact on robustness and recovery.
________________________________________
6. Criticality, Disasters, and Risk-Based Analysis
Identify critical components and evaluate extreme scenarios.
•	Criticality: Automated analysis ranking the importance of network components. Combines graph theory and hydraulic impact assessment to identify elements whose failure would cause the largest service disruption.
•	Disasters: Model large-scale external events (earthquakes, floods). Define event extent and intensity to simulate correlated failures and assess cascading effects on water supply for emergency planning.
________________________________________
7. Zone Creation and Zone-Based Analysis
Enable spatial and logical partitioning of the network for detailed management.
•	Create Zones: Define network zones (District Metered Areas, pressure zones). Draw boundaries manually, generate automatically based on topology, or import from GIS files.
•	Analyze Zones: Provides a dedicated dashboard for zone-based performance analysis. Calculates KPIs like average zone pressure, night flow, and non-revenue water estimates.
•	Specific Zone: Focused analysis for a single selected zone. Isolates the zone for simulations, visualizations, and edits, enabling efficient localized investigation.
________________________________________
8. Incremental Element and Feature Management
Quick, dynamic modifications to network complexity.
•	Add More: Incrementally add common network elements (junctions, pipes, tanks, sources, curves or patterns). Users can click on the map to place elements; software suggests logical connections.
•	Remove More: Quickly remove network elements. Click individual elements or draw a selection area to delete multiple items. Confirmation and warnings prevent unintended disconnections.




AquaStream360 Modeling Module: Middle Frame (Main Toolbar) Documentation
The Middle Frame is the central command toolbar positioned prominently within the Modeling interface. It contains essential, frequently-used tools for system navigation, workspace management, simulation execution, and rapid access to core analysis features. This toolbar enables users to efficiently control their workflow without navigating through nested menus.
________________________________________
1. Interface Control & Navigation
Tools for managing the application workspace, screen layout, and returning to major application areas.
•	Welcome Scrn: Returns the user to the main application welcome screen.
•	Units: Opens the system units configuration and guide, explaining AquaStream360's unit conventions and allowing comparison with standards like EPANET for verification.
•	Left Pannel: Toggles the visibility of the left-side input/output panel, allowing users to maximize screen real estate for the map or visualization area.
•	Right Pannel: Toggles the visibility of the right-side analysis and visualization controls panel.
•	Expand: Maximizes the currently active central view (map, graph, or 3D scene) to a near full-screen state for detailed, unobstructed inspection.
•	Round Off: Opens a settings popup to configure the numerical precision (decimal places or significant figures) used for displaying all results throughout the application.
________________________________________
2. Geospatial & Network Utilities
Tools for spatial calculations, coordinate configuration, and network property examination.
•	Distance Calc: Launches a geodetic and mathematical distance calculator for measuring paths between points, useful for field planning and spatial verification.
•	Loops: Identifies and highlights all links where the start node is the same as the end node, and provides an option to remove them if needed.
•	Set UTM Zone: Configures the Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) coordinate zone for accurate georeferencing and spatial analysis when working with projected map data.
•	Locate: Opens a search tool to find a specific network element (pipe, junction, tank) by ID and automatically zooms to its location on the map.
________________________________________
3. Core Simulation & Reporting
Primary controls for running the hydraulic engine and generating outputs.
•	Simulate: Runs the hydraulic (and water quality) simulation for the current network model. Unlocks subsequent analysis features.
•	Report: Generates and exports a professional report containing key network results, graphs, tables, and map images.
•	Speech: Checkbox to toggle optional audio guidance; provides spoken hints and instructions when enabled.
________________________________________
4. Demand & Economic Analysis
Tools focused on demand calculation, population data, and cost assessment.
•	Population: Analyzes the network population at each node, which can be calculated based on the demand characteristics defined in the network.
•	Demand Analysis: Compares simulated water demand against expected or design values, highlighting under- or over-supplied areas.
•	Demand/capita: Set average water demand per person (cubic meters per capita per day) for the network or selected zones.
•	Cost Calc: Estimates capital, operational, and annual costs based on materials, energy, and maintenance.
________________________________________
5. Integration & Scenario Tools
Features for exporting data and testing network modifications.
•	Testing: Opens a sandbox environment to safely test "what-if" scenarios (different pipe sizes, pump settings) without affecting the main model.
•	AutoCAD: Exports CAD-compatible drawing files (DWG) from the current network model for engineering purposes.
•	Check Affected: After a network modification or failure simulation, identifies which elements, customers, or zones are impacted.
________________________________________
6. Visualization & Advanced Analysis
Buttons providing access to graphical and mapping modules for exploring results. All these buttons open a dedicated, feature-rich dialogue with its own controls and guides for analysis.
•	Topography: Opens DEM importer and 3D terrain analysis tools for ground elevations and site topography analysis.
•	3D Flows: Launches 3D visualization to explore network geometry and animated flow patterns.
•	Flow Anim: Plays animated visualization of flow directions and magnitudes over the 2D network map.
•	Flow Analysis: Advanced module for in-depth hydraulic analysis of flow rates, velocities, and directions.
•	Path Finder: Traces and visualizes the shortest or most critical flow path from a source (e.g., tank) to a selected network point.
•	Net Results: Displays all raw numerical simulation results (pressures, flows, heads) in a sortable table.
•	Pie Charts: Generates pie charts for categorical data (e.g., pipe materials, demand by type).
•	Analysis: Entry point to the broader Visual Analysis suite of graphing and plotting tools.
•	Transformation: Apply geometric transformations (translation, rotation, scaling) to selected network parts.
•	Pressure Map: Generates a color-coded thematic map visualizing pressure values across all junctions.
•	Full 3D Map View: Opens immersive full-screen 3D view combining network model with terrain and buildings.
•	Graph Anims: Creates animated time-series graphs showing parameter changes (tank level, pressure) over simulation time.
•	Risk Analysis: Performs and visualizes risk assessment based on failure probability and consequence.
•	Flow Map: Creates a thematic map where pipe segments are colored and labeled according to flow values.
•	Corr Heatmap: Generates a correlation heatmap for exploring relationships between network variables.
•	Quality Investigator: Opens water quality module for tracking constituents like chlorine or water age.
•	Box plots: Creates box-and-whisker plots for key network variables (e.g., pressure distribution across nodes).
•	Elementary: Opens detailed element-by-element  analysis for inspecting individual pipe  performance.




















The left panel of the AquaStream360 Modeling section (when Drawing Mode is off) contains: 
Network Input & Creation Panel (Right Side)
This panel provides multiple pathways for creating or loading a water distribution network model. It supports everything from loading pre-built examples and industry-standard formats to advanced import options for raw or unstructured data. All options are designed to be highly automated, requiring minimal user configuration.
________________________________________
1. Primary Import & Creation Methods
•	CREATE NET FROM INPUT DATA LAYERS:
The primary automated import tool. Opens a file browser allowing the user to select a folder containing multiple data files of various supported formats (e.g., CSV, Excel, Shapefile, KML, GeoJSON). This functionality corresponds to the Network Builder feature.
•	Load Example Network:
Instantly loads a pre-built, fully functional example network. This allows new users to explore features immediately .
•	Manual Entries Form:
Opens a step-by-step form for manually defining all network components (junctions, pipes, tanks, pumps, etc.) and their attributes without drawing or importing files. Ideal for small networks or highly controlled manual input.
________________________________________
2. Loading Previously Created Networks
•	Recently loaded Net:
Quickly reloads the most recently used network from the current session.
•	Show Recent Networks:
Displays a list or gallery of recently opened network models and their date of modifications for fast access.
•	Load Pickle File:
Loads a network model saved in AquaStream360’s native .pkl (Python pickle) format. This preserves the complete internal state of the model, including custom attributes and prior analysis results.
________________________________________
3. Import from Standard Engineering Formats
•	EPANET INP:
Imports network models from the industry-standard .inp file format used by EPANET, ensuring full compatibility with existing hydraulic models.
•	Excel WorkBook:
Imports network data from structured Microsoft Excel (.xlsx or .xls) files. The system interprets specific worksheets or named ranges to identify pipes, nodes, pumps, and their properties.
•	JSON Files:
Imports network data from .json files, commonly used for structured data exchange in modern analytical and web-based workflows.
•	Geo JSON Files:
Imports geospatial network data from the GeoJSON format, a standard for encoding geographic features using JSON, ideal for GIS-integrated modeling.
•	GIS Shapefiles:
Imports network geometry and attributes from ESRI Shapefiles (.shp, .shx, .dbf), one of the most widely used geospatial data standards.
________________________________________
4. Import from Unstructured & Survey Data
•	Unstructured Data:
Imports a single CSV or Excel file containing mixed network element types (pipes, junctions, pumps, etc.) in one table, similar to raw field or survey data. The system automatically detects, classifies, and separates elements to build the model.
•	CSV Geodataframes:
Imports spatial network data from CSV files formatted as geodataframes, where geometry is encoded in a column, enabling direct spatial model creation.
________________________________________
5. Manual Drawing & Creation Tools
•	Draw on Map:
Activates interactive drawing directly on the GIS map. Users can place junctions and draw pipes, with automatic snapping to geographic coordinates.
•	Drawing Mode:
Opens a free-form drawing canvas for sketching a network schematic without geographic reference, useful for conceptual or preliminary design.
•	Draw From Image:
Allows users to trace over a background image (such as a scanned map, schematic diagram, or satellite image) to digitize and create a network model.
________________________________________
6. Advanced & Specialized Import
•	Append Model:
Imports additional elements from a data file and adds them to an existing network model, enabling incremental expansion or updates without rebuilding the project.
•	Model Dictionary:
Imports a network model from a Python dictionary structure, supporting programmatic model generation and integration with custom scripts or external tools.














AquaStream360 Modeling Module: Left Panel Interactive Controls
This panel contains controls for configuring how users interact with the network model on the main map screen. These settings define selection behavior, editing modes, visual styles, and the scope of analysis when clicking or dragging on the network.
________________________________________
1. Primary Interaction Mode
Sets the fundamental behavior for user interaction with network elements.
•	INTERACTIVE MODE: When enabled, the software provides immediate visual feedback, and analysis when the user hovers over or clicks network elements on the screen. This is the default and recommended mode for most workflows.
________________________________________
2. Editing & Analysis Mode Selection
Radio buttons that switch the panel between two fundamental purposes.
•	Editing Mode: Activates tools for modifying the network's physical structure. Users can add, delete, move, and draw new pipes and nodes directly on the map.
•	Analysis Mode: Focuses on investigating the existing network. Clicking on elements displays their simulation results, properties, and performance data instead of allowing geometric edits.
________________________________________
3. Element Selection Focus
Defines which types of network components can be selected with the mouse in Editing or Analysis Mode.
•	Selectable Nodes: Mouse clicks highlight and interact with junctions, tanks, and reservoirs (the points in the network).
•	Selectable Links: Mouse clicks highlight and interact with pipes, pumps, and valves (the connecting lines in the network).
________________________________________
4. Advanced Editing & Analysis Options
Checkboxes that enable specialized functions during interaction.
•	Relocate Elements: In Editing Mode, allows users to click and drag any selected node to a new location; connected links adjust automatically.
•	Attribute Trend Analysis: I n Analysis Mode, clicking a link element performs stress and failure probability analysis for that element and generates various crucial graphics, including a comparative trend graph of its attributes or results along its length.
•	Delete Nodes Per Click: A safety/precision toggle. When checked, a single click deletes a node (and connected links). When unchecked, a confirmation dialog appears.
•	Include Pipe Hydraulics and Internal Stress Analysis: In Analysis Mode, the results panel includes advanced mechanical stress calculations (like hoop stress) alongside standard hydraulic results (pressure, flow).
________________________________________
5. Bulk Operations & Visual Style
Controls for large-scale edits and changing the visual representation of the network.
•	Line Style (Links): Dropdown to change the visual appearance of all pipes/links. Options include Solid, Dashed, Dotted, and varying thickness styles.
•	Delete Many: Opens a menu for bulk deletion. Options typically include deleting all disconnected elements or all elements inside a user-drawn polygon.
•	SizeSlider: Horizontal slider (0–50) controlling visible elements sizes.












AquaStream360 Modeling Module: Network Output & Export Panel
This panel provides a complete suite of tools for persisting work, exporting data, and distributing results. It supports a wide range of formats tailored for engineering design, GIS workflows, data science, reporting, and cross-platform collaboration.
________________________________________
1. Primary Export & Save Methods
•	CONVERT NETWORK TO RAW DATA LAYERS:
The primary bulk export mechanism. Saves the current network into a folder containing multiple separated data files (such as CSV, GeoJSON, and metadata files). This process effectively reverses the automated Network Builder import and creates a portable, vendor-neutral dataset that can be re-imported to fully reconstruct the model.
•	UTM Pickle File:
Saves the complete AquaStream360 network model to the native .pkl format, preserving all custom attributes, simulation results, zones, and internal state. Coordinates are automatically converted to UTM to ensure spatial consistency. This is the recommended format for saving active or in-progress projects.
•	Geo-Referenced (.inp):
Exports the network to the standard EPANET .inp format while embedding geospatial coordinate information.
________________________________________
2. Export to GIS & Geospatial Formats
•	Geo JSON:
Exports network geometry and attributes to the GeoJSON format, suitable for web mapping platforms and modern GIS environments.
•	GIS DataFrames:
Exports the network to structured CSV or similar files formatted as spatial dataframes, optimized for use in Python-based workflows (Pandas, GeoPandas) and other analytical environments.
•	Shapefile Files:
Exports the network to ESRI Shapefile format, the most widely supported GIS vector standard, ensuring compatibility with ArcGIS, QGIS, and other GIS software.
•	Save HTML Map:
Generates a standalone interactive HTML map. The file can be opened in any modern web browser and shared without requiring AquaStream360.
________________________________________
3. Export to Engineering & CAD Formats
•	AutoCAD (DXF):
Open a dialogue that allows the user to set properties and exports network geometry, including labels and information table to the DXF format for direct use in AutoCAD and other CAD platforms, enabling detailed engineering drawings and annotations.
•	CSV Files:
Exports structured network element data (pipes, nodes, pumps, etc.) to simple CSV files for universal compatibility and spreadsheet-based analysis.
•	Microsoft Excel WB:
Exports the network into a structured Microsoft Excel workbook, organizing different element types into separate worksheets for clarity and ease of review.
________________________________________
4. Export to Databases & Programmatic Formats
•	PostgreSQL Database:
Uploads the network model directly into a PostgreSQL/PostGIS database, supporting enterprise-level storage, spatial querying, versioning, and multi-user collaboration.
•	JSON:
Exports the network to a generic JSON format, ideal for integration with custom scripts, APIs, web applications, or automated pipelines.
________________________________________
5. Export for Reporting & Documentation
•	Print (PDF):
Generates printable PDF outputs of the current network view, simulation results, maps, and charts.
•	Microsoft Word:
Exports structured network summaries and results into a Microsoft Word document for inclusion in formal reports and technical documentation.
•	Image Files:
Exports the current map or visualization to standard image formats (PNG, JPEG) for presentations, reports, and external publications.
________________________________________






















AquaStream360 Modeling Module: Network Management & History Controls

This horizontal toolbar contains essential controls for managing the lifecycle of the active network model. Positioned at the top of the left panel, these tools allow users to control project state, manage naming, reset progress, and navigate through the complete history of changes made during modeling and analysis.
________________________________________
1. Network State Management
Core controls for modifying, resetting, or removing the current network.
•	🗑️ Remove Net:
Permanently deletes the entire current network model from the workspace. This action removes all elements, attributes, zones, and simulation results, returning the project to a blank state. A confirmation dialog is presented to prevent accidental data loss.
•	📝 Rename:
Opens a dialog allowing the user to rename the active network model. This is especially useful for organizing multiple scenarios, alternatives, or design iterations within the same project.
•	🔄 Reset:
Restores the network to its original imported or created state. All edits, simulations, and modifications made after the initial load are discarded. This feature is ideal for abandoning experimental changes and restarting an analysis from a clean baseline.
________________________________________
2. Action History & Undo / Redo
Advanced tools for navigating and managing the project’s full change history.
•	Action History:
Opens a dedicated visual timeline showing every action performed on the network, including edits, imports, deletions, and simulations. Each entry records the action type, timestamp, and resulting model state. Users can select any point in the timeline to instantly revert the network to that exact state, enabling efficient scenario exploration and precise recovery from mistakes.
•	⬅ Undo (Ctrl + Z):
Reverses the most recent action applied to the network, providing immediate correction of unintended changes.
•	➡ Redo (Ctrl + Y):
Reapplies the most recently undone action, allowing users to move forward again through the project’s history.






















