AquaStream360: Network Zoning Page
This page is designed for creating, managing, and analyzing Network Zones, which are geographic or logical subdivisions of your water distribution network (such as District Metered Areas - DMAs). Zones are critical for pressure management, leakage control, and operational performance analysis.
AquaStream360 provides both manual and automated methods for defining zones. All zones, regardless of how they are created, can be managed and analyzed from this page. The Zone Analysis module, accessible from the top bar, offers detailed performance metrics and comparison tools.
Note: Manual creation here complements the Create Zones feature, which can automatically generate optimized zones based on hydraulic and topographic data. Both approaches are fully integrated: zones appear here for management and their analysis results populate the same display area.
________________________________________
1. Define Zone Boundaries
Zones are defined geographically using one of three methods:
•	Draw Boundaries on Map: Activate a drawing mode on the GIS view. Click to create a polygon around the area for the zone. This is the most intuitive method for manual definition.
•	Specify Locations: Manually input a list of geographic coordinates:
o	A table appears where you can Add Row for each boundary point.
o	Enter Name, Latitude, and Longitude for each point.
o	Click Get Zone From The Table to generate the zone from your listed points.
o	Click Save All Zones to store your definitions.
•	Import Boundaries: Load predefined zone boundaries from external GIS files (Shapefile, GeoJSON). Ideal for integrating planning documents or prior studies.
________________________________________
2. Work with Defined Zones
Once zones are created, they can be managed, visualized, and attached to the network:
•	Visualize Boundaries: Overlay the zones on the map for easy inspection.
•	Show Nodes in Each Zone: Highlights network junctions (nodes) within each zone.
•	Attach Zones to Network: Assigns a Zone ID attribute to all elements (nodes and pipes) based on their geographic location. This is essential for any zone-based analysis.
•	Save All Zones: Export zone geometries to a file (Excel, Shapefile, or GeoJSON) for later use or sharing.
________________________________________
3. Analyze Zones
While the full analysis is performed in the Zone Analysis module, core functions are accessible from this page:
•	Zone Analysis: Opens the dedicated module where you can:
o	Calculate average, minimum, and maximum pressures per zone.
o	Analyze total demand or leakage estimates for each zone.
o	Compare performance metrics across zones.
Prerequisites for Analysis:
1.	Run a hydraulic simulation for your network.
2.	Ensure zones are attached to the network using the button in section 2.
________________________________________
4. Information Panel
The right-side panel serves as your central log and guidance area:
•	Displays step-by-step instructions for manual zone creation.
•	Shows elements per zone and other results from zone operations.
•	Provides notes, warnings, and key concepts to help you understand zoning workflows.
Tip: Whether you use manual methods or automated tools like Create Zones, the panel consistently shows results and provides actionable feedback.


AquaStream360: Water Quality Page 
This page is dedicated to setting up and running Water Quality Simulations. It allows modeling of three analysis types: Water Age, Tracer Studies, and Chemical Concentration. You can configure sources, set simulation parameters, and run animations to visualize how water quality evolves throughout your network over time.
The interface is context-sensitive: input panels only appear when the corresponding analysis type is selected.
________________________________________
1. Select Water Quality Analysis Type
Choose the type of water quality study you want to perform. Your selection determines which configuration options become available.
•	Water Age: Models travel time through the network to detect potential stagnation zones. (Default selection)
•	Tracer: Tracks a conservative (non-reactive) substance to study flow paths and travel times.
•	Chemical Concentration: Models transport and decay of reactive chemicals (e.g., chlorine, contaminants).
Water Quality Investigator: Opens the dedicated Water Quality Investigator module. This advanced tool provides detailed tracking, visualization, and analysis beyond the basic simulation configuration.
________________________________________
2. Configure the Analysis (Context-Sensitive Panels)
A. Tracer Analysis
When Tracer is selected, the Tracer Options panel appears:
•	Parameter: Confirms the analysis type (TRACE).
•	Trace Node: Specify the junction where the tracer is injected.
•	Diffusivity: Set the tracer’s molecular diffusivity (default: 1.0).
•	Tolerance: Set solver tolerance for the quality calculation (default: 0.01).
•	Apply / Clear: Save or reset tracer settings.
B. Chemical Concentration Analysis
When Chemical Concentration is selected, the Add a Source panel appears:
•	Source Name: Unique identifier for the chemical injection (e.g., Spill_At_Plant).
•	Injection Node Name: Specify the network node where the chemical enters.
•	Source Type: Injection method: Concentration, Mass, Flowpaced, or Setpoint.
•	Initial Quality: Define the starting concentration or mass.
•	Pattern Name: Optional time-based pattern for variable injection.
•	Start Time / End Time: Define the active period for the source.
•	Apply: Adds the configured source to the network model for simulation.
________________________________________
3. Run and Visualize
After configuring your analysis:
•	ANIMATE: Runs the water quality simulation and displays results as an animation on the network plot. Visualize concentration, age, or tracer spread dynamically.
•	Additional Water Quality Settings: Opens a dialog for more granular simulation controls.
•	Time Options: Adjust simulation duration and reporting timesteps for the water quality analysis.
________________________________________
4. Information Panel
The right-side panel provides:
•	Guidance on setup and parameter selection.
•	Real-time feedback and warnings.
•	Summaries of network elements affected by sources or analysis outcomes.
