AquaStream360: Options Page
The Options Page allows you to configure settings that affect how your network is analyzed, visualized, and reported. It is organized into five main categories:
1.	Locations – Map your network to real-world coordinates
2.	Simulation Time – Control simulation duration and timesteps
3.	Hydraulic – Adjust pressure and flow calculations
4.	Energy – Set pump operating costs
5.	Reaction – Configure water quality chemical reactions
________________________________________
How to Navigate
On the left panel, click a category to access its settings. Each section contains input fields, dropdowns, and buttons to save or apply changes.
________________________________________
1. LOCATIONS – Map Your Network
This section is for placing your network on a map and calculating accurate pipe lengths.
Reference Points
•	Point A / Point B: Enter two known locations with names and coordinates (decimal degrees, e.g., -17.8262, 31.0520)
•	Georeference: Apply these coordinates to position the entire network
Automatic Distance Calculations
•	Get Suggested Points: Finds the two furthest points in your network
•	Calculation Method:
o	UTM (Auto-Zone) – Recommended for local networks
o	Geodetic (pyproj) – Accurate for long distances
o	Geodesic (Geopy) – Similar accuracy to pyproj
o	Haversine – Fast, approximate method
Special Features
•	Calibration with Known Measurements: Scale distances using measured pipe lengths
•	Auto-calculate Pipe Lengths: Compute distances for all pipes
•	Set Map Zoom: Save your preferred map view
•	Set UTM Zone: Manually specify if needed (default: 36S)
Tip: Use UTM for local systems, Geodetic for cross-region networks. Always verify critical distances.
________________________________________
2. SIMULATION TIME – Control Analysis Duration
These settings define how long the simulation runs and how often results are calculated.
Main Time Settings
•	Duration – Total simulation time (default: 7 days = 604,800 seconds)
•	Hydraulic Timestep – Frequency of pressure/flow calculations (default: 15 min)
•	Quality Timestep – Frequency of water quality updates (default: 15 min)
•	Pattern Timestep – Frequency of demand pattern changes (default: 1 hr)
Report File Settings
•	Report Timestep – How often results are saved in exported EPANET reports
•	Report Start – When to begin saving results
•	Statistic – Choose summary type (average, minimum, etc.)
•	Pattern Interpolation – Smooths demand changes over time
Quick Actions
•	Default Time Options – Reset to recommended values
•	Apply Changes – Save settings
•	Clear – Empty all fields
Note: Report settings only affect exported EPANET files; they do not change what you see in AquaStream360.
________________________________________
3. HYDRAULIC – Pressure & Flow Settings
Controls how the software calculates pressure and flow throughout your network.
Network-Wide Settings
•	Headloss Formula – Select pipe friction calculation method:
o	H-W: Hazen-Williams (default, common)
o	D-W: Darcy-Weisbach (accurate for all flows)
o	C-M: Chezy-Manning (alternative method)
•	Fluid Properties – Viscosity and specific gravity (default: 1.0 for water)
•	Maximum Calculations – Number of solver iterations (default: 200)
•	Solution Accuracy – Convergence threshold (default: 0.001)
Pressure-Dependent Demand (PDD)
•	Demand Model – Switch between standard (DD) and PDD
•	Minimum Pressure – Below this, demand is reduced
•	Required Pressure – Needed for full demand delivery
Adjust Junction-Specific Settings
•	Select a junction to override default pressures
•	Enter Minimum Pressure, Required Pressure, and Exponent
All pressures are in meters of water column.
________________________________________
4. ENERGY – Pump Costs
Set electricity pricing and pump efficiency for cost analysis.
•	Default Price – Cost per kWh
•	Price Pattern – Time-varying electricity rates
•	Pump Efficiency – Average efficiency of pumps
•	Demand Charge – Additional cost for peak usage
Use these settings only if calculating energy expenses.
________________________________________
5. REACTION – Water Quality
Configure chemical decay in your network (e.g., chlorine).
Reaction Rates
•	Bulk Water – Decay in the water itself
•	Pipe Wall – Reaction with pipe materials
•	Tank Reactions – Behavior in storage tanks
Reaction Strength
•	Bulk Coefficient – Rate of water-phase reactions
•	Wall Coefficient – Rate of wall-phase reactions
Defaults are sufficient for most users; adjust only if modeling water quality decay.
________________________________________
WHEN TO USE THESE SETTINGS
Typical Users:
•	Locations – Map network or calculate pipe lengths
•	Simulation Time – Adjust analysis duration
Advanced Users:
•	Hydraulic – Troubleshoot pressure solver issues
•	Energy – Calculate pump operating costs
•	Reaction – Model chemical decay
Quick Tips:
•	Start with Locations for map alignment
•	Use Default Options if unsure
•	Most defaults work well for standard networks
•	Only modify advanced settings if needed

