AquaStream360: Filter & Query Panel
The Filter & Query Panel, located at the top-right of the interface, provides powerful tools for searching, filtering, and interrogating your water network. It allows you to ask both static questions (about model data) and dynamic questions (about simulation results).
Think of it as a search engine and investigation console for your water system.
Each tab is designed around a specific type of question or analysis workflow.
________________________________________
TAB 1: INSTANT QUERIES
Quick, direct questions about network element properties.
Question Pattern:
“At [Element Type] named [Element Name], query the attribute [Attribute]”
How to Use
1.	Element Type – Select Reservoir, Tank, Junction, Pipe, Valve, or Pump
2.	Element Name – Choose the specific element from the dropdown
3.	Attribute – Select the property to retrieve (options depend on element type)
4.	Answer – Click to display the value instantly
Typical Examples
•	At Junction named J-23 query the attribute Elevation
•	At Pipe named P-45 query the attribute Diameter
•	At Tank named T-12 query the attribute Initial Level
Best for: Quick inspections, spot checks, and verifying model data.
________________________________________
TAB 2: SIMULATED ATTRIBUTES
Query simulation results at a specific time.
⚠️ Requires a completed simulation.
Question Pattern:
“[Attribute] at [Element Type] named [Element Name] after [Time] hours”
How to Use
1.	Attribute – Choose a result type
(Demand, Flowrate, Head, Velocity, Quality, Headloss, Pressure)
2.	Element Type – Select the category
3.	Element Name – Choose the element
4.	Time – Select a reporting time from the dropdown
5.	Answer – Displays the result at that simulation time
Typical Examples
•	Pressure at Junction named J-15 after 24 hours
•	Flowrate at Pipe named P-32 after 12 hours
•	Quality at Tank named T-5 after 48 hours
Best for: Time-based performance checks without opening plots or animations.
________________________________________
TAB 3: PERFORMANCE INVESTIGATION
Access to system-wide diagnostic tools.
Available Tools
•	Investigator Button – Opens the Network Performance Investigator
•	Comprehensive Network Analysis – One-click entry into advanced evaluation workflows
When to Use
•	System performance assessment
•	Identifying bottlenecks or anomalies
•	Preparing reports or presentations
This tab serves as a gateway to deep analytical modules, rather than individual queries.
________________________________________
TAB 4: CONNECTIONS
Explore how network elements are connected.
Question Pattern:
“At [Node Type] named [Element Name], what are the connected links?”
How to Use
1.	Element Type – Junction, Tank, or Reservoir
2.	Element Name – Select the node
3.	Attribute – Currently: Connected links
4.	Answer – Lists all connected pipes, pumps, and valves
Additional Tool
•	Nodes With Many Connections – Identifies highly connected nodes (potential critical junctions)
Example
•	At Junction named J-18, what are connected links?
Best for: Understanding topology, redundancy, and critical connection points.
________________________________________
TAB 5: THRESHOLD-BASED QUERIES
Find elements that meet specific criteria.
Question Pattern:
“Which [Element Type] has [Attribute] [Comparison] [Value]”
How to Use
1.	Element Type – Any network element
2.	Attribute – Property to evaluate
3.	Comparison – ≥, =, ≠, >, <, ≤, between, outside
4.	Value – Numeric threshold(s)
5.	Answer – Lists all matching elements
6.	Locate – Highlights results on the map
Typical Examples
•	Which Junction has Elevation ≥ 100
•	Which Pipe has Diameter < 0.3
•	Which Tank has Initial Level between 5 and 10
Best for: Identifying risk zones, compliance checks, and screening large networks.
________________________________________
TAB 6: FILTER (EXTREMES)
Find elements with largest or smallest values.
Question Pattern:
