AquaStream360 – Disaster Analysis Section (Page 4)
Complete UI & Functional Documentation
(Analysis Tab – Disaster Modeling Workspace)
________________________________________
1. Overview
The Disaster Analysis Section enables users to model, simulate, and analyze extreme, abnormal, and emergency conditions affecting the water distribution network.
This section supports single-event and multi-event cascading disaster scenarios, allowing users to understand system resilience, vulnerabilities, and recovery behavior.
All disaster simulations are executed against temporary analytical states, preserving the integrity of the original network unless explicitly overridden.
________________________________________
2. Disaster Scenario Definition Panel (Left Panel)
This panel contains predefined disaster templates. Selecting a scenario activates its configuration workflow.
2.1 Infrastructure Failure Scenarios
Pipe Break or Leak
•	Function: Models a rupture, crack, or structural failure in a pipe
•	User Inputs:
o	Pipe selection
o	Break or leak size
o	Start time during simulation
•	Purpose: Assess pressure loss, flow redistribution, and service disruption
Node Leak
•	Function: Models leakage at a junction, tank, or reservoir
•	User Inputs:
o	Node selection
o	Leak flow rate
o	Start time
•	Purpose: Evaluate localized demand loss and pressure impacts
Blockage
•	Function: Simulates complete obstruction in a pipe
•	User Inputs:
o	Pipe selection
o	Degree of diameter reduction or full closure
•	Purpose: Analyze capacity loss and rerouting effects
________________________________________
2.2 Operational and Utility Failures
Power Outage
•	Function: Simulates electrical failure affecting pumps and powered valves
•	User Inputs:
o	Affected pumping stations
o	Outage duration
•	Purpose: Evaluate system behavior under loss of active control
Water Shedding
•	Function: Implements controlled, scheduled supply reduction
•	User Inputs:
o	Affected zones
o	Timing schedule
•	Purpose: Plan demand management and emergency rationing strategies
Unauthorized Alterations
•	Function: Models illegal connections or physical tampering
•	User Inputs:
o	Altered components
o	Modified demands or operational parameters
•	Purpose: Investigate non-technical losses and security vulnerabilities
________________________________________
2.3 Emergency and Environmental Events
Fires
•	Function: Adds high, short-term firefighting demands
•	User Inputs:
o	Fire locations
o	Required flow rate
o	Event duration
•	Purpose: Validate fire flow capacity and pressure resilience
Environmental Change
•	Function: Modifies global system parameters
•	User Inputs:
o	Demand pattern multipliers
o	Source water quality changes
•	Purpose: Simulate droughts, seasonal shifts, or climate effects
Contamination
•	Function: Injects harmful substances into the network
•	User Inputs:
o	Injection location
o	Contaminant type and concentration
o	Injection duration
•	Purpose: Analyze spread, exposure, and detection behavior

Earthquake
Typical Inputs
•	Earthquake Magnitude (Mw)
Used to estimate ground motion intensity.
•	Epicenter Location (X, Y coordinates)
Determines distance from each pipe to the earthquake source.
•	Ground Motion Model (GMPE)
Converts magnitude and distance into peak ground velocity (PGV) or peak ground acceleration (PGA).
•	Pipe Fragility Parameters
o	Pipe material
o	Diameter
o	Repair rate coefficients (e.g., breaks per km as a function of PGV)
•	Minimum Damage Thresholds (optional)
Defines when damage begins to occur.

Intentional Disruption
•	Function: Simulates coordinated sabotage or vandalism
•	User Inputs:
o	Multiple critical components
•	Purpose: Evaluate system robustness against deliberate attacks
Other Disaster Scenarios
•	Function: Fully customizable disaster builder
•	User Inputs:
