AquaStream360 – Resilience Analysis Section (Page 6)
________________________________________
1. Purpose of the Resilience Analysis Section
The Resilience Analysis Section provides an environment for evaluating the ability of a water distribution network to withstand, absorb, adapt to, and recover from stresses and disturbances.
This section enables decision-makers to assess resilience using topological, hydraulic, water quality, security, and economic metrics, all within a unified analytical framework.
The section supports:
•	Quantitative resilience assessment
•	Comparative analysis across multiple resilience dimensions
•	Interpretation of results through integrated explanations
•	Visualization, reporting, and export of analytical outputs
________________________________________
2. Interface Layout and Overall Workflow
The interface is organized into two primary zones, designed to guide users through a structured and logical analytical workflow.
2.1 Left Zone – Analysis Configuration and Metric Selection
This zone is dedicated to:
•	Selecting resilience metric categories
•	Executing specific resilience calculations
•	Configuring global parameters
•	Reviewing contextual explanations of selected metrics
2.2 Right Zone – Results Display and Interaction
This zone is dedicated to:
•	Displaying analysis results in tabular form
•	Enabling visualization and exploration of outputs
•	Exporting results for external use
•	Supporting post-analysis interpretation
The interface emphasizes discoverability, contextual guidance, and professional usability, ensuring that complex resilience concepts are accessible without oversimplification.
________________________________________
3. Left Zone – Analysis Configuration and Metric Selection
________________________________________
3.1 Metric Category Selection Panel
Located in the upper-left portion of the interface and labeled “Selected Metrics:”, this panel allows the user to define the resilience dimension to be analyzed.
Available Metric Categories 
•	Topographic Metrics (default selection)
Focus on network structure, connectivity, redundancy, and graph-theoretic properties.
•	Hydraulic Metrics
Focus on hydraulic performance, system capacity, pressure adequacy, and demand satisfaction.
•	Water Quality Metrics
Focus on water age, chemical concentrations, and water quality impacts.
•	Water Security Metrics
Focus on contamination exposure, spread, and population impact.
•	Economic Metrics
Focus on cost, energy consumption, and environmental impact.
Selecting a category dynamically updates the available analysis tools in the panel below and configures the calculation engine to operate within the chosen resilience domain.
________________________________________
3.2 Metric-Specific Analysis Panels
Below the category selector is a stacked analysis panel. The content of this panel changes based on the selected metric category.
________________________________________
3.2.1 Topographic Metrics Panel
This panel provides graph-theory-based analysis tools that describe the structural resilience of the network.
Available analyses include:
•	Node Degree – Number of connected pipes at each junction
•	Terminal Nodes – Identification of dead-end junctions
•	Bridges – Links whose removal would disconnect the network (displayed with hydraulic attributes)
•	Articulation Points – Nodes whose removal would disconnect the network
•	Betweenness Centrality – Importance of nodes/links in shortest paths
•	Closeness Centrality – Accessibility of nodes within the network
•	Diameter – Longest shortest path in the network
•	Eccentricity – Maximum distance from a node to all others
•	Simple Paths – Enumeration of possible routes between two points
•	Shortest Path Lengths – Minimum distances between network elements
•	Link Density – Ratio of existing connections to possible connections
•	Valve Segmentation – Partitioning of the network using isolation valves
•	Valve Segment Attributes – Characteristics of the resulting isolated segments
________________________________________
3.2.2 Hydraulic Metrics Panel
These metrics require a prior hydraulic simulation and focus on operational performance.
Available analyses include:
•	Node Attributes – Pressure, demand, and head at junctions
•	Link Attributes – Flow, velocity, and headloss in pipes
•	Todini Index – Classical resilience index based on surplus hydraulic energy
•	Modified Resilience Index – Enhanced formulation of hydraulic resilience
•	Entropy – Measure of flow distribution diversity and reliability
•	Tank Capacity – Storage availability and utilization
•	Expected Demand – Theoretical demand prior to simulation
•	Actual Demand – Delivered demand obtained from simulation
•	Average Expected Demand – System-wide benchmark demand
•	Water Service Availability – Ratio of delivered to required demand
•	Population Impacted – Estimated population affected by service deficiencies
•	Visual Demand Analysis – Visual comparison of expected versus delivered demand
________________________________________
3.2.3 Water Quality Metrics Panel
This panel provides water quality and age-related analyses. Some options are noted as under development.
Available analyses include:
•	Water Age – Water age at individual nodes
•	Average Water Age – System-wide water age statistics
•	Chemical Concentration – Contaminant or disinfectant levels at nodes
•	Average Chemical Concentration – System-wide chemical statistics
•	Trace – Tracking of contaminant movement through the network
•	Average Trace – Aggregate contaminant travel behavior
•	Population Impacted – Population affected by water quality issues
•	Average Population Impacted – System-wide population statistics
•	Investigate Nodes Quality – Detailed node-level quality analysis
________________________________________
3.2.4 Water Security Metrics Panel
This panel supports contamination exposure and security-related resilience assessment.
Available analyses include:
•	Mass Consumed – Contaminant mass consumed by users
•	Volume Consumed – Volume of contaminated water consumed
•	Extent of Contamination – Geographic spread of contaminants
•	Population Impacted – Population exposed to contamination
•	Detection Limit Configuration – Displays current detection limit (e.g., “Detection limit is 0 mg”)
•	Reset Detection Limit – Allows configuration of contaminant detection thresholds
•	Future Feature – Placeholder for upcoming security tools
________________________________________
3.2.5 Economic Metrics Panel
This panel evaluates economic and energy-related resilience aspects.
Available analyses include:
•	Network Cost – Capital and installation costs
•	Greenhouse Gas Emissions – Environmental impact of system operation
•	Pump Operating Power – Instantaneous power requirements
•	Pump Operating Energy – Total energy consumption
•	Pump Operating Cost – Monetary cost of energy use
